@@@@ open-cpu-scheduling-policies
situation: open
topic: CPU scheduling: FCFS, SJF and round-robin
voice: plain
level: medium
expect: {"must":["round-robin","waiting time"],"askMustNot":["policy A (is|has|gives|wins)","\\b20 ms","\\b29\\b"]}
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

The scheduler decides which runnable thread gets a core next, and that choice sets both how long jobs wait and how quickly a system responds. Linux, the Go runtime's goroutine scheduler and every batch queue make the same trade: finish work early on average, or keep every task responsive. The three textbook policies (first come first served, shortest job first, round-robin) each settle that trade differently, and the measures used to compare them, waiting time and turnaround time, are the ones you will use when sizing thread pools and queue workers. They are also the base for multi-level feedback queues and fair-share scheduling.

<ask type="predict">Three jobs arrive together and each needs 10 ms of CPU. Policy A runs each job to completion in turn. Policy B is round-robin with a 1 ms time slice. Under which policy is the average time until a job finishes lower, and why?</ask>
@@ verify
# the hidden answer to the probe: run to completion finishes at 10, 20, 30; round-robin at 28, 29, 30
a = [10, 20, 30]
rem, t, done = [10, 10, 10], 0, {}
while len(done) < 3:
    for i in range(3):
        if rem[i]:
            rem[i] -= 1; t += 1
            if rem[i] == 0: done[i] = t
assert [done[i] for i in range(3)] == [28, 29, 30]
assert sum(a) / 3 == 20 and sum(done.values()) / 3 == 29
# neither figure is given away
assert '29' not in reply and '20 ms' not in reply

