@@@@ open-refresh-link-counts
situation: open
topic: Hard links and symbolic links
options: {"refresh":true}
voice: plain
level: easy
expect: {"setsProblem":true,"must":["link count"],"askMustNot":["No such file","\\b2, 2\\b","dangling"],"mustNot":["Why this matters"]}
@@ reply
**Problem 1**

A directory holds one file, `report.txt`, whose inode has a link count of 1. These three commands are run in order:

```
ln report.txt copy.txt
ln -s report.txt short.txt
rm report.txt
```

<solution>ln adds a second directory entry for the same inode, so the count becomes 2. ln -s creates a separate inode that stores the path "report.txt"; the count of the original stays 2. rm removes one entry, so the count falls to 1 and the data stays. At the end cat copy.txt prints the contents, and cat short.txt fails with "No such file or directory" because the stored path no longer resolves.</solution>

<ask type="problem">What is the inode's link count after each of the three commands, and what do `cat copy.txt` and `cat short.txt` do at the end?</ask>
@@ verify
import os, tempfile
with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
    p = lambda n: os.path.join(d, n)
    open(p('report.txt'), 'w').write('data\n')
    assert os.stat(p('report.txt')).st_nlink == 1
    os.link(p('report.txt'), p('copy.txt'))
    c1 = os.stat(p('report.txt')).st_nlink
    os.symlink('report.txt', p('short.txt'))
    c2 = os.stat(p('report.txt')).st_nlink
    os.remove(p('report.txt'))
    c3 = os.stat(p('copy.txt')).st_nlink
    assert (c1, c2, c3) == (2, 2, 1)
    assert open(p('copy.txt')).read() == 'data\n'
    try:
        open(p('short.txt')).read(); raise AssertionError('the symlink still resolves')
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
s = solution(reply)
assert 'becomes 2' in s and 'stays 2' in s and 'falls to 1' in s and 'No such file or directory' in s

@@@@ open-prior-semaphore-count
situation: open-prior
topic: Semaphores
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Semaphores","daysAgo":4,"finished":false,"plan":["Counting semaphores: wait and signal","Binary semaphores as locks","The bounded buffer with two semaphores"],"right":3,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["A semaphore starts at 2. Three threads call wait() and none has called signal(). How many are blocked?"],"open":"In the bounded buffer, why does the producer wait on the 'empty' semaphore before taking the mutex?"}]}
expect: {"must":["semaphore","bounded"],"askMustNot":["\\b(three|3) (of them )?(get|pass)","\\b(two|2) (are |stay |left )?blocked"]}
noverify: an opening; its question (3 pass, 2 block) is asked, not answered
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A semaphore is a counter that the kernel or a threading library guards for you. It is how a connection pool admits only N users at once, how a bounded queue makes producers wait when it is full, and how one thread tells another that work is ready. You have wait and signal and the binary case from last time; the counting behaviour slipped once, and the bounded buffer, which depends on it, is still to come.

<ask type="short">A semaphore is initialised to 3. Five threads each call wait() once, and no thread calls signal(). How many of the five get past wait(), and how many are left blocked?</ask>

@@@@ open-prior-rwlock-from-mutex
situation: open-prior
topic: Reader-writer locks
voice: plain
level: medium
before: {"prior":[{"topic":"Mutex locks","daysAgo":6,"finished":true,"plan":["Critical sections","Lock and unlock around shared data","Contention and its cost"],"right":4,"partly":1,"wrong":1,"missed":["Two threads only read a shared map and never write it. Is a mutex needed for correctness?"]}]}
expect: {"must":["mutex","read"],"askMustNot":["no data race","cannot see a wrong","no lock is needed"]}
noverify: an opening; conceptual, nothing computed
@@ reply
**Why this matters**

A reader-writer lock lets any number of threads read shared data at once and gives a writer exclusive access. It suits data that is read far more often than it is changed: a routing table, a configuration snapshot, an in-memory cache. `pthread_rwlock_t`, Go's `sync.RWMutex` and C++'s `std::shared_mutex` are the forms you will meet. It rests on one idea from your mutex lesson: which combinations of threads can touch shared data at the same moment without harm. That was the question you missed there, so it comes first.

<ask type="short">Four threads call `lookup(key)` on a shared table at the same time, and no thread modifies the table while they run. Can any of them get a wrong result because of the others? Say why or why not.</ask>

